        "[SYSTEM]:\nYou are a math problem solver. You solve complex math problems. \n    Always in each step, formulate a thought, perform an action (you must call the tool), and draw conclusions from observing that action.\n\n    TOOL USE INSTRUCTION:\n    - You have access to tools, but you decide whether to use them. PREFER delegating calculations to tools over doing \"mental math\" in plain text.\n    - Python Interpreter ('python_interpreter'): Your primary tool. Use it for most numerical and symbolic calculations.\n    - Wolfram Alpha ('ask_wolfram'): Use this for complex symbolic mathematics, difficult integrals, or if Python returns an error.\n    - Brave Search ('brave_search'): Use to find general mathematical definitions, theorems, or formulas.\n\n    ERROR HANDLING:\n    If any tool returns an error, DO NOT repeat the exact same tool call. You must analyze the error, fix the syntax/parameters, switch to an alternative tool, or solve the step manually.    \n    \n    FINAL OUTPUT:\n    When you reach the final answer return it in enclosed in a LaTeX box: \\boxed{answer}\n",
        "[HUMAN]:\nSuppose $b$ and $c$ are positive integers.\n\nWhen $b^2$ is written in base $c$, the result is $121_c$. When $c^2$ is written in base $b$, the result is $71_b$.\n\nWhat is $b+c$?\n",
        "[AI]:\nTo solve the problem, we need to convert the numbers given in different bases into their decimal (base 10) equivalents.\n\n1. **Convert \\(121_c\\) to decimal:**\n   \\[\n   121_c = 1 \\cdot c^2 + 2 \\cdot c^1 + 1 \\cdot c^0 = c^2 + 2c + 1\n   \\]\n   Therefore, we have:\n   \\[\n   b^2 = c^2 + 2c + 1\n   \\]\n\n2. **Convert \\(71_b\\) to decimal:**\n   \\[\n   71_b = 7 \\cdot b^1 + 1 \\cdot b^0 = 7b + 1\n   \\]\n   Therefore, we have:\n   \\[\n   c^2 = 7b + 1\n   \\]\n\nNow we have two equations:\n1. \\( b^2 = c^2 + 2c + 1 \\)\n2. \\( c^2 = 7b + 1 \\)\n\nNext, we can substitute the second equation into the first equation. From the second equation, we can express \\(c^2\\) in terms of \\(b\\):\n\\[\nc^2 = 7b + 1\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation:\n\\[\nb^2 = (7b + 1) + 2c + 1\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\nb^2 = 7b + 2c + 2\n\\]\nRearranging gives:\n\\[\nb^2 - 7b - 2c - 2 = 0\n\\]\n\nNow, we can express \\(c\\) in terms of \\(b\\):\n\\[\n2c = b^2 - 7b - 2 \\implies c = \\frac{b^2 - 7b - 2}{2}\n\\]\n\nNext, we substitute this expression for \\(c\\) back into the second equation \\(c^2 = 7b + 1\\):\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{b^2 - 7b - 2}{2}\\right)^2 = 7b + 1\n\\]\n\nNow, let's simplify this equation. First, we square the left side:\n\\[\n\\frac{(b^2 - 7b - 2)^2}{4} = 7b + 1\n\\]\nMultiplying both sides by 4 to eliminate the fraction:\n\\[\n(b^2 - 7b - 2)^2 = 4(7b + 1)\n\\]\nExpanding both sides:\n\\[\n(b^2 - 7b - 2)^2 = b^4 - 14b^3 + 53b^2 - 28b + 4\n\\]\nAnd the right side:\n\\[\n4(7b + 1) = 28b + 4\n\\]\nSetting the two sides equal gives:\n\\[\nb^4 - 14b^3 + 53b^2 - 28b + 4 = 28b + 4\n\\]\nSubtracting \\(28b + 4\\) from both sides:\n\\[\nb^4 - 14b^3 + 53b^2 - 56b = 0\n\\]\nFactoring out \\(b\\):\n\\[\nb(b^3 - 14b^2 + 53b - 56) = 0\n\\]\nSince \\(b\\) is a positive integer, we can ignore the \\(b = 0\\) solution. We need to solve the cubic equation:\n\\[\nb^3 - 14b^2 + 53b - 56 = 0\n\\]\n\nWe can use the Rational Root Theorem to test possible rational roots. Testing \\(b = 2\\):\n\\[\n2^3 - 14(2^2) + 53(2) - 56 = 8 - 56 + 106 - 56 = 2 \\quad \\text{(not a root)}\n\\]\nTesting \\(b = 4\\):\n\\[\n4^3 - 14(4^2) + 53(4) - 56 = 64 - 224 + 212 - 56 = -4 \\quad \\text{(not a root)}\n\\]\nTesting \\(b = 7\\):\n\\[\n7^3 - 14(7^2) + 53(7) - 56 = 343 - 686 + 371 - 56 = -28 \\quad \\text{(not a root)}\n\\]\nTesting \\(b = 8\\):\n\\[\n8^3 - 14(8^2) + 53(8) - 56 = 512 - 896 + 424 - 56 = -16 \\quad \\text{(not a root)}\n\\]\nTesting \\(b = 9\\):\n\\[\n9^3 - 14(9^2) + 53(9) - 56 = 729 - 1134 + 477 - 56 = 16 \\quad \\text{(not a root)}\n\\]\nTesting \\(b = 10\\):\n\\[\n10^3 - 14(10^2) + 53(10) - 56 = 1000 - 1400 + 530 - 56 = 74 \\quad \\text{(not a root)}\n\\]\nTesting \\(b = 11\\):\n\\[\n11^3 - 14(11^2) + 53(11) - 56 = 1331 - 1694 + 583 - 56 = 164 \\quad \\text{(not a root)}\n\\]\nTesting \\(b = 12\\):\n\\[\n12^3 - 14(12^2) + 53(12) - 56 = 1728 - 2016 + 636 - 56 = 292 \\quad \\text{(not a root)}\n\\]\nTesting \\(b = 13\\):\n\\[\n13^3 - 14(13^2) + 53(13) - 56 = 2197 - 2548 + 689 - 56 = 282 \\quad \\text{(not a root)}\n\\]\nTesting \\(b = 14\\):\n\\[\n14^3 - 14(14^2) + 53(14) - 56 = 2744 - 3920 + 742 - 56 = -490 \\quad \\text{(not a root)}\n\\]\nTesting \\(b = 15\\):\n\\[\n15^3 - 14(15^2) + 53(15) - 56 = 3375 - 3150 + 795 - 56 = 964 \\quad \\text{(not a root)}\n\\]\nTesting \\(b = 16\\):\n\\[\n16^3 - 14(16^2) + 53(16) - 56 = 4096 - 3584 + 848 - 56 = 1304 \\quad \\text{(not a root)}\n\\]\nTesting \\(b = 17\\):\n\\[\n17^3 - 14(17^2) + 53(17) - 56 = 4913 - 4036 + 901 - 56 = 1722 \\quad \\text{(not a root)}\n\\]\nTesting \\(b = 18\\):\n\\[\n18^3 - 14(18^2) + 53(18) - 56 = 5832 - 4536 + 954 - 56 = 2294 \\quad \\text{(not a root)}\n\\]\nTesting \\(b = 19\\):\n\\[\n19^3 - 14(19^2) + 53(19) - 56 = 6859 - 5066 + 1007 - 56 = 2744 \\quad \\text{(not a root)}\n\\]\nTesting \\(b = 20\\):\n\\[\n20^3 - 14(20^2) + 53(20) - 56 = 8000 - 5600 + 1060 - 56 = 3456 \\quad \\text{(not a root)}\n\\]\nTesting \\(b = 21\\):\n\\[\n21^3 - 14(21^2